          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had reflux for years and now food sometimes sticks going down. How worrying is that, and what does guidance describe about when to investigate?",
          "answer": "Food sticking when you swallow is a symptom worth raising with a doctor. Reflux does not usually cause it on its own. The coding reference (**coding-gerd-k21**) says dysphagia (difficulty swallowing) \"is not routinely part of uncomplicated reflux disease, and where it is documented it is reported in addition.\" That means it is treated as a separate finding that needs its own assessment.\n\nThe material I retrieved does not say when or how dysphagia should be investigated. It does not describe which tests are used, or which signs should prompt urgent review. I can't fill that gap from general knowledge, so please ask a clinician.\n\nMy safety check found no emergency pattern in your description. That does not mean there is no concern. The check only covers a fixed list of presentations. Seek medical advice promptly if the sticking is getting worse, is new or unexplained, or happens with other changes you notice. Seek urgent care right away if you cannot swallow your own saliva, or if you have chest pain or trouble breathing.\n\nWhen you see a clinician, describe how long this has been happening, whether it affects solids, liquids or both, and whether you have had any unintended weight loss.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
